<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heidelberg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heidelberg is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It was the residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> palatine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> from the 13th century until 1720, and its university, founded by Rupert I in 1386, is the oldest in present-day Germany. Its castle was wrecked by French troops in 1689 and 1693 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a>, and the ruins became an emblem of German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heidelberg.jpg" alt="Heidelberg" width="320" height="169" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jörg Braukmann · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%B6nigstuhl,_Heidelberg,_U-17.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
